      "text": "On a Mac, Docker stores components in two different \"Library\" locations. If you are looking specifically for the **system-level** `/Library` folder (available to all users), the folders are primarily used for background services and privileged helpers.\n\n### 1. System Library: `/Library`\nThe following paths are where Docker stores its system-wide background components:\n\n*   **Privileged Helpers:** `/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/`\n    *   Look for files like `com.docker.vmnetd` and `com.docker.socket`. These allow Docker to manage networking and the system socket.\n*   **Launch Daemons:** `/Library/LaunchDaemons/`\n    *   Look for `com.docker.vmnetd.plist` or `com.docker.socket.plist`. These tell macOS how to start the Docker background services.\n*   **Application Support:** `/Library/Application Support/com.docker.docker/`\n    *   This folder sometimes contains system-wide configuration files like `registry.json` or certificates.\n\n---\n\n### 2. User Library: `~/Library` (Most common)\nIf you are looking for your **actual Docker data** (images, containers, and volumes), it is located in your **user\u0027s** Library, not the system one. To get there, open Finder, press `Cmd + Shift + G`, and paste:\n\n`~/Library/Containers/com.docker.docker/Data/vms/0/data/`\n\nInside this folder, you will see a large file named **`Docker.raw`** or **`Docker.qcow2`**. This file is a virtual disk that contains your entire Docker environment.\n\n### Summary Table\n| Data Type | Path |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Containers \u0026 Images** | `~/Library/Containers/com.docker.docker/Data/vms/0/data/Docker.raw` |\n| **Privileged Services** | `/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/com.docker.vmnetd` |\n| **Startup Plists** | `/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.docker.vmnetd.plist` |\n| **Config \u0026 Metadata** | `~/.docker/` (hidden folder in your Home directory) |\n| **Application Logs** | `~/Library/Logs/Docker Desktop/` |\n\n**Note:** If you are trying to free up space, do not delete files in `/Library` manually. Instead, use the **\"Troubleshoot\"** (bug icon) menu within the Docker Desktop app to \"Clean / Purge data\" or \"Uninstall.\"",
