      "text": "Yes, one of them is almost certainly the reason your disk is full. \n\nTo be clear: the folders in the **system-level** `/Library` (the ones I listed first) are almost never the problem. They contain tiny helper tools. The \"culprit\" is in your **user-level** `~/Library`.\n\n### The \"Space Hog\": `Docker.raw`\nThe file taking up all your space is located here:\n`~/Library/Containers/com.docker.docker/Data/vms/0/data/Docker.raw`\n\n**Why it is so big:**\nDocker on Mac doesn\u0027t run natively; it runs inside a lightweight Linux Virtual Machine. macOS sees that entire virtual machine—including every image you\u0027ve pulled, every container you\u0027ve run, and every volume you\u0027ve created—as **one single massive file** (`Docker.raw`).\n\n*   **The \"Illusion\" of Size:** By default, Docker Desktop often reserves **64GB** for this file. Even if you only have 5GB of actual data, Finder might show it as a 64GB file. This is called a \"sparse file.\"\n*   **The Reality:** Even though it\u0027s a sparse file, as you use Docker, it will grow until it hits that 64GB (or whatever limit you set) and it **does not automatically shrink** back down when you delete images.\n\n---\n\n### How to check what is actually inside it\nInstead of looking at the folder in Finder, run this command in your Terminal to see what is eating the space inside that file:\n\n```bash\ndocker system df\n```\n\nThis will give you a breakdown of:\n*   **Images:** Old versions of projects or base images (like `node`, `python`, `ubuntu`) you pulled months ago.\n*   **Containers:** Data stored in the \"writable layer\" of containers.\n*   **Local Volumes:** Database files or persistent data from your projects.\n*   **Build Cache:** Temporary files created while building images (this is often the biggest hidden space eater).\n\n---\n\n### How to reclaim the space\nIf that folder is too large, **do not delete the file manually** in Finder (this can crash Docker). Instead:\n\n1.  **The \"Quick Clean\":** Run this command to delete all unused data (stopped containers, unused networks, and \"dangling\" images):\n    ```bash\n    docker system prune -a --volumes\n    ```\n2.  **The \"Deep Clean\" (Settings):**\n    *   Open **Docker Desktop**.\n    *   Click the **Bug Icon** (Troubleshoot) in the top right.\n    *   Click **Clean / Purge data**.\n3.  **The Permanent Fix (Resize):**\n    *   Go to **Settings** (Gear icon) \u003e **Resources**.\n    *   Lower the **Disk image size** slider. \n    *   *Note: Shrinking this limit will usually delete your existing images and containers, so back up what you need first.*\n\n**Summary:** If you see a massive folder in `/Library` or `~/Library`, it is that `Docker.raw` file. It\u0027s essentially a \"virtual hard drive\" that gets cluttered over time.",
