        "text": "Yes, one of them is almost certainly the reason your disk is full. \n\nTo be clear: the folders in the **system-level** `/Library` (the ones I listed first) are almost"
      }, {
        "text": " never the problem. They contain tiny helper tools. The \"culprit\" is in your **user-level** "
      }, {
        "text": "`~/Library`.\n\n### The \"Space Hog\": `Docker.raw`\nThe file taking up all your space is located here"
      }, {
        "text": ":\n`~/Library/Containers/com.docker.docker/Data/vms/0/data/Docker.raw"
      }, {
        "text": "`\n\n**Why it is so big:**\nDocker on Mac doesn\u0027t run natively; it runs inside a lightweight Linux"
      }, {
        "text": " Virtual Machine. macOS sees that entire virtual machine—including every image you\u0027ve pulled, every container you\u0027ve run, and every"
      }, {
        "text": " volume you\u0027ve created—as **one single massive file** (`Docker.raw`).\n\n*   **The \""
      }, {
        "text": "Illusion\" of Size:** By default, Docker Desktop often reserves **64GB** for this file. Even if you only"
      }, {
        "text": " have 5GB of actual data, Finder might show it as a 64GB file. This is called a \"sparse file"
      }, {
        "text": ".\"\n*   **The Reality:** Even though it\u0027s a sparse file, as you use Docker, it will grow"
      }, {
        "text": " until it hits that 64GB (or whatever limit you set) and it **does not automatically shrink** back down"
      }, {
        "text": " when you delete images.\n\n---\n\n### How to check what is actually inside it\nInstead of looking at the folder in Finder"
      }, {
        "text": ", run this command in your Terminal to see what is eating the space inside that file:\n\n```bash\ndocker system df\n"
      }, {
        "text": "```\n\nThis will give you a breakdown of:\n*   **Images:** Old versions of projects or base images (like "
      }, {
        "text": "`node`, `python`, `ubuntu`) you pulled months ago.\n*   **Containers:** Data stored in the \""
      }, {
        "text": "writable layer\" of containers.\n*   **Local Volumes:** Database files or persistent data from your projects.\n*   **Build"
      }, {
        "text": " Cache:** Temporary files created while building images (this is often the biggest hidden space eater).\n\n---\n\n### How to"
      }, {
        "text": " reclaim the space\nIf that folder is too large, **do not delete the file manually** in Finder (this can"
      }, {
        "text": " crash Docker). Instead:\n\n1.  **The \"Quick Clean\":** Run this command to delete all unused data"
      }, {
        "text": " (stopped containers, unused networks, and \"dangling\" images):\n    ```bash\n    docker system prune -"
      }, {
        "text": "a --volumes\n    ```\n2.  **The \"Deep Clean\" (Settings):**\n    *   Open **"
      }, {
        "text": "Docker Desktop**.\n    *   Click the **Bug Icon** (Troubleshoot) in the top right.\n"
      }, {
        "text": "    *   Click **Clean / Purge data**.\n3.  **The Permanent Fix (Resize):**\n    *"
      }, {
        "text": "   Go to **Settings** (Gear icon) \u003e **Resources**.\n    *   Lower the **Disk image size** slider"
      }, {
        "text": ". \n    *   *Note: Shrinking this limit will usually delete your existing images and containers, so back"
      }, {
        "text": " up what you need first.*\n\n**Summary:** If you see a massive folder in `/Library` or `~/Library"
      }, {
        "text": "`, it is that `Docker.raw` file. It\u0027s essentially a \"virtual hard drive\" that gets cluttered"
      }, {
        "text": " over time."
