      "text": "The fact that your report shows **0B** for everything means you have no active or hidden data *inside* Docker, yet the `Docker.raw` file is likely still sitting on your hard drive taking up 16GB, 64GB, or more.\n\nThis happens because of how Docker on Mac works: it creates a \"virtual disk\" that **grows but never shrinks.**\n\n### Why is it still big?\n1.  **The \"High-Water Mark\" Problem:** Even if you delete every image and container you own, the `Docker.raw` file stays at the largest size it ever reached. If you once had 40GB of images and deleted them, the file remains 40GB.\n2.  **Pre-allocation:** By default, Docker Desktop \"claims\" a certain amount of space from macOS (usually 64GB). Even if it’s empty, macOS may see that space as \"reserved\" or \"used\" depending on how you\u0027re checking your disk.\n\n---\n\n### How to reclaim the space (Since you have 0B usage)\nSince your `docker system df` is empty, you won\u0027t lose anything by doing a \"hard reset\" of the disk image. This is the only way to shrink that file back to zero.\n\n#### Option A: The \"Shrink\" Method (Recommended)\nThis forces Docker to recreate the file at a smaller size.\n1.  Open **Docker Desktop**.\n2.  Go to **Settings** (Gear icon) \u003e **Resources**.\n3.  Look for the **Disk image size** slider.\n4.  Move it to a smaller size (e.g., 16GB or 32GB) and click **Apply \u0026 Restart**.\n    *   *Note: This will delete the old file and create a new, tiny one.*\n\n#### Option B: The \"Purge\" Method\nThis keeps your current limit but wipes the file clean.\n1.  Click the **Bug Icon** (Troubleshoot) in the top-right of Docker Desktop.\n2.  Click **Clean / Purge data**.\n3.  Select everything and confirm.\n\n#### Option C: The \"Nuclear\" Method\nIf the file is still there and you want it gone immediately:\n1.  **Quit Docker Desktop** completely.\n2.  In Terminal, run:\n    ```bash\n    rm ~/Library/Containers/com.docker.docker/Data/vms/0/data/Docker.raw\n    ```\n3.  Restart Docker Desktop. It will see the file is missing and create a brand new, empty one (starting at around 1-2GB).\n\n### Summary\n`docker system df` tells you what\u0027s happening **inside** the suitcase. The file size in `/Library` tells you how big the **suitcase itself** is. Since your suitcase is empty, you should just \"replace the suitcase\" (Option A or C) to get your Mac\u0027s disk space back.",
