      "text": "If you’ve already deleted Docker and the `Docker.raw` file, the \"hidden space\" you are still seeing is likely caused by one of two things: **APFS Local Snapshots** (the most common reason) or **Docker leftovers** in other hidden folders.\n\n### 1. The Most Likely Culprit: APFS Local Snapshots\nWhen you delete a massive file (like `Docker.raw`), macOS often doesn\u0027t actually release the disk space immediately. Instead, it \"locks\" those blocks in a **Local Snapshot** so that Time Machine can restore them if needed. \n\nEven if you don\u0027t use an external backup drive, macOS creates these snapshots automatically. The space is marked as \"Purgeable,\" but it won\u0027t show up as \"Free\" in many views.\n\n**How to fix it:**\n1.  Open **Terminal**.\n2.  List your current snapshots by typing:\n    ```bash\n    tmutil listlocalsnapshots /\n    ```\n3.  If you see a list of dates, you can force macOS to delete them and reclaim the space immediately by running:\n    ```bash\n    sudo tmutil thinlocalsnapshots / 9999999999 4\n    ```\n    *(You will need to enter your Mac password. This command \"thins\" the snapshots to free up as much space as possible.)*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Manual Cleanup of Docker Leftovers\nDocker leaves files in several other hidden locations besides the main `Library` folder. If you just dragged the app to the Trash, these are still on your disk:\n\nOpen **Terminal** and run these commands to remove any remaining Docker data (Copy and paste one by one):\n\n```bash\n# Configuration and Build Cache\nrm -rf ~/.docker\n\n# Metadata and Sockets\nrm -rf ~/Library/Group\\ Containers/group.com.docker\n\n# Application Support\nrm -rf ~/Library/Application\\ Support/Docker\\ Desktop\n\n# Caches\nrm -rf ~/Library/Caches/com.docker.docker\n\n# Logs\nrm -rf ~/Library/Logs/Docker\\ Desktop\n\n# Preferences\nrm -f ~/Library/Preferences/com.docker.docker.plist\nrm -f ~/Library/Saved\\ Application\\ State/com.electron.docker-frontend.savedState\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Check for \"Ghost\" Virtual Machines\nIf you ever used alternative Docker tools (like **Colima**, **OrbStack**, or **Minikube**), they create their own massive disk images in different hidden folders.\n\n*   **Colima:** Check `~/.colima`\n*   **OrbStack:** Check `~/Library/Application Support/OrbStack`\n*   **Minikube:** Check `~/.minikube`\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to \"See\" the Hidden Space\nIf the space still isn\u0027t back, you can use the Terminal to find exactly which folder is the problem. Run this command to see the size of all folders in your User Library (it may take a minute to calculate):\n\n```bash\ndu -sh ~/Library/* | sort -h\n```\n\n**Look for any line ending in \"G\" (Gigabytes).** This will tell you exactly which folder is still hogging your space.\n\n**Final Tip:** Don\u0027t forget to **Empty the Trash** and **Restart your Mac**. macOS sometimes requires a reboot to recalculate the \"Available Space\" accurately after a large deletion.",
